{
  "id": "dativ",
  "name": "Indirect object pronouns: le doy, se lo doy",
  "level": "A2",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "variety": "es-ES",
  "es419": { "note": "Same pronouns in Latin America, without os (os digo): there people say les digo for “you all”." },
  "subtitle": "Indirect object pronouns say who gets something: me, te, le, nos, os, les. Le doy el libro a Ana. When le or les meets lo, la, los or las, it becomes se: Se lo doy.",
  "briefMarks": ["me", "te", "le", "nos", "os", "les", "se lo"],
  "source": "icanspeak Spanish original text (grammar-a2 run, 2026-11-04)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      { "text": "me · te · le · nos · os · les", "variant": "prep" },
      { "text": "lo · la · los · las" },
      { "text": "doy · digo · envío", "variant": "verb" }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">Le</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">doy</span> el papel a Ana. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Me</span> <span class=\"vp-verb\">explica</span> el contrato. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Te</span> lo <span class=\"vp-verb\">envío</span> mañana. · <span class=\"vp-prep\">Se</span> lo <span class=\"vp-verb\">doy</span> a Ana.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "The indirect object is the person who <strong>receives</strong> something or for whom something happens: <em>Le doy el dinero. Nos explica el horario. Te digo la verdad.</em>",
        "tag": "Who gets it"
      },
      {
        "html": "The pronoun goes <strong>before the conjugated verb</strong>, with <em>no</em> in front: <em>No me dice nada.</em> With an infinitive it can also go on the end: <em>Quiero darle esto. = Le quiero dar esto.</em>",
        "tag": "Position"
      },
      {
        "html": "Spanish usually <strong>says the pronoun and the person together</strong>: <em>Le doy el libro a Ana. Les escribo a mis padres.</em> This is normal, not a repetition mistake.",
        "tag": "le + a Ana"
      },
      {
        "html": "With two pronouns, the <strong>person comes first</strong>: <em>Me lo da. Te la envío. Nos los traen.</em>",
        "tag": "Person first"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>le</strong> and <strong>les</strong> can't stand before <em>lo, la, los, las</em>. They become <strong>se</strong>: <em>Le doy el libro → Se lo doy. Les envío la carta → Se la envío.</em>",
        "tag": "le → se"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Common mistakes: ✗ <em>Le lo doy</em> → ✓ <em>Se lo doy</em>. ✗ <em>Lo me da</em> → ✓ <em>Me lo da</em>. ✗ <em>Doy le el libro</em> → ✓ <em>Le doy el libro</em>. ✗ <em>La digo la verdad</em> → ✓ <em>Le digo la verdad</em>, also for a woman. For the person or thing the verb acts on directly, see <a href=\"/object-pronouns\">direct object pronouns</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
      "tables": [
